WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.220
Pitch, hit, and run like the pros, bass is loaded!

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:27.560
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:28.780
I'm Mike.

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:29.700
I'm Joe.

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:38.680
And I'm Sean. And today we are talking about Jaleco's Bases Loaded, a baseball game, but I understand that,

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:42.660
you know, not everyone here might be familiar with baseball.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:47.140
So, I mean, in order for you to understand what we're talking about with this game, you

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:50.360
sort of have to understand what the sport of baseball is even about.

00:00:50.360 --> 00:01:00.800
So, basically, baseball is their weekly reenactments of the Battle of Gettysburg, where there were

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:04.660
the Union troops who stood on top of large hills.

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:05.500
Were they large?

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:07.340
They were. It was a very large hill.

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:13.520
And then the Confederate troops stood out in a wide field.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:19.400
And they had sticks and balls and basically would throw a ball up into the air and hit it

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:25.160
with a stick. And if it hit the person that threw the ball, well, that's a strike.

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:28.340
Baseball is also a numbers game.

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:29.560
It's a game of numbers.

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:30.980
It's a game of math magicians.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:35.640
It's a game for everybody who can count.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:37.720
Because ultimately, yeah, count down.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:39.560
Down? Mostly up.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.160
Yeah, I actually can't think of a scenario in baseball where you count down.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:43.600
Well, how many outs you have left.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:45.480
Yeah, you do everything in reverse.

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:47.520
And how many more runs you have until 100.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:50.140
Until 100. From 100.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:55.200
You know, in true fashion though, no Major League Baseball game has ever scored 100 runs.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:56.300
Ever. How do you know?

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:58.520
Combined? I just know.

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:02.500
I think actually like the most combined runs ever was something like 36.

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:05.140
That's stupid. It's a lot of runs in one game though.

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:08.020
Yeah. Anyway, that's not what we're here to talk about.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:11.100
Thank you for your very accurate description of baseball, Sean.

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:16.540
I'm sure if you listened to last week's episode, Faithful Fans, you already know what

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:17.540
baseball is all about.

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:20.240
It's about the American Revolutionary War.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:21.780
Not the Civil War.

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:28.160
And we are going to get into that right away as we talk about what makes Jaleco's bases

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:34.360
loaded. So much that more different than the other three baseball games that we've played so far.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:38.820
And I will take it away with the easiest description I can give you, which is that somebody has

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:44.320
finally figured out a way to take what you're seeing on your TV when you watch a baseball game

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:49.660
and then say, hey, why not just make it look like that while they play the baseball game?

00:02:49.660 --> 00:03:06.880
It seems that somebody in the art department at Jaleco finally took a class in human figure drawing because they actually do look like human beings instead of these very weird pudgy meatball men that also are wearing glaring hats and jerseys.

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:12.000
Yeah. And the camera is definitely coming from center field where it is for all the game feeds.

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:17.820
There's perspective. And yeah. And you see yourself on the pitcher's mound and it's facing the batter.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:20.060
And that doesn't change when you're at bat.

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:25.020
You're still seeing the same view that you would see if you were watching a Major League

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:33.300
baseball game. So this is the first time that we're really seeing a kind of like a rewrite on how to do a baseball game on the NES.

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:35.540
And this is kind of, I mean, it's like the norm now too, right?

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:36.760
I mean, it's like you're watching it on TV.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:48.420
I mean, this is actually a very good old timey looking thing that you would get if you were pitching in a game, like in a current game.

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:50.720
But if you're batting, it's still behind you.

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:54.380
That's right. I guess you could play it with you behind the pitcher.

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:55.820
I actually never tried that.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:02.200
Yeah. Usually in games today, you're only ever like basically from the catcher's perspective.

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:08.180
Yeah. But this looks like an HD remaster of the entire sport of baseball.

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:16.360
I got to say, the graphics in this game are so choice, whether it's the batting screens or the fielding screens.

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:20.800
They just look so tasty and scrumptious and like 70s mustaches everywhere.

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:25.160
Yeah, it's a little like, I don't even know what to add to that, but you're right.

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:38.400
Yeah. And there's also like, while we're talking about the presentation of everything, there's something here that all of the other games were missing for me in terms of like flair to these things.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:39.600
Like you can see. Like Rick Flair.

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:45.300
Yeah, exactly. But you can see like people behind you in the stadium.

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:47.020
Yeah. You can see like.

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:49.280
Well, there are like little dots in the last game.

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:53.420
The umpires get like, the umpires get names even, like for no good reason.

00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:59.460
It goes back to the scoreboard to show you like the entire lineups and who's coming up to bat.

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:04.840
If you get a home run, you get a little, you get a cool little animatic.

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:08.880
Yeah. Of the person rounding the bases behind the base.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:20.100
And also, you know, for those of you who are familiar with like baseball in the 80s and stuff like that, that's how minimal the scoreboard used to be too.

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:25.020
So like, it's kind of like the on screen, the on screen, the constant on screen scoreboard.

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:28.300
It like having like ball strikes outs right there and just showing you them.

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:32.000
Like it feels, it feels very much alive in this one.

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:33.520
I like, I like the presentation.

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:41.060
They stepped it up tenfold from the only thing that's really missing from like a real factor is the catcher's mitt.

00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:42.340
What's going on there?

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:46.480
This is next gen graphics here.

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:50.040
Like, first of all, they didn't need to put the catcher's mitt in the game.

00:05:50.220 --> 00:06:00.300
The fact that they put it in, I know it's a weird thing that if it's way out, if it's, if it's way out of the strike zone, if he, the guy kind of loses his mitt.

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:06.460
But the fact that it's in there at, it has like a, it has a purpose gameplay wise.

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:10.180
Um, it's just a, I think a really cool addition.

00:06:10.460 --> 00:06:16.240
To elaborate on that a little bit, uh, the, what the catcher's mitt does is it, it basically moves where you're pitching the ball.

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:20.620
So you can better see as the pitcher or as the batter, where the ball is going.

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:22.420
Yeah. So it's a nice feature.

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:24.340
It does kind of triangulate the position.

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:25.760
And it does. Yes.

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:29.760
It does float a little ways off the catcher's body and go way outside.

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:33.040
It's almost like it's disembodied from him because he doesn't move at all.

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:34.180
And I'm not expecting him to.

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:39.980
So before anybody comes back, I was never expecting the catcher to actually like bend his knees a little and stuff like that.

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:44.660
I just thought that was like a funny, like touch to like all this other, like great stuff that's going on.

00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:47.760
However, all made up teams and players.

00:06:48.700 --> 00:06:52.300
This isn't, this isn't a game breaker for me, or this isn't a deal breaker for me.

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:55.740
I love games, uh, sports games that create that.

00:06:55.740 --> 00:07:04.720
I mean, they don't, this isn't created its own universe, but it does have like stories kind of told between the names of players and what teams they're on.

00:07:04.900 --> 00:07:08.880
Sort of like reminds me of, uh, Blitz the League.

00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:13.640
If you've ever played that, um, it's, I mean, it's, it's, it's a game.

00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:14.980
It's not anything like this.

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:22.200
It's first of all, it's football, but it is, uh, made up teams with made up players and, uh, they all have their own little stories to tell.

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:23.840
And this is sort of like that.

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:25.140
It's fun. Yeah.

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:28.120
I definitely didn't mean it as like a deal breaker either.

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:34.200
I just thought that it's interesting how like we saw one game that did get the team names.

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:35.520
The other one that got the player names.

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:37.740
And this time it's just like, yeah, you know what?

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:38.800
Yeah. None of that.

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:40.100
But instead, Utah.

00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:41.480
Yeah. I was just going to say Utah.

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:42.820
It's so funny.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:49.340
Right. But instead you get to choose like, um, you know, what, what factor you want in your game.

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:52.960
So now, now in 1980 alone, we've had three different baseball games.

00:07:52.960 --> 00:08:01.620
So it's either you care about your team, you care about your players, your athletes, or you just care about presentation of what a baseball game is.

00:08:01.660 --> 00:08:03.880
Because a lot of people like to just like watch baseball.

00:08:03.880 --> 00:08:05.000
It's therapeutic, you know?

00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:10.880
Like, it's a long game that, um, some people recently have not, you know, have been trying to like speed up.

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:14.260
But for everything baseball is, I think that, uh, this one is very faithful.

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:20.740
Yeah. That adaptation. And I was surprised on booting the game up because I never looked at anything beforehand.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:23.940
I was surprised to see this graphical.

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:30.720
Yeah. So was I. Not even just the graphical presentation, but just the, like the decisions on where the camera, quote unquote, the camera is and everything.

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:34.080
I was, did not expect them to change up the formula that much.

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:45.760
Yeah. They're really, they're really going for something here too, because immediately, uh, you can, you can see that you can do a whole, uh, 162 game season of, of, um, of baseball.

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:48.320
I think it's a nine game season, but.

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:53.720
Oh, really? I guess I never, like, I never asked myself like what a season would entail.

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:54.820
I think that's like a round robin.

00:08:54.880 --> 00:08:56.000
But, okay. That's cool.

00:08:56.240 --> 00:08:57.400
You play, yeah, you play. You play the other team.

00:08:57.640 --> 00:09:04.900
Yeah. Okay. Because I was going to say that, uh, the password system there, um, gives you the option to like.

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:06.200
Yeah. Store your progress.

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:07.960
Store your progress and stuff like that.

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:11.540
And the other team's records, because after every game it'll give you a box score.

00:09:11.900 --> 00:09:23.640
Yeah. So, uh, it was just, it was just neat to see like something that would actually like store stats in that way, rather than just provide the stats of players based on like, you know, the set stats that they provide.

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:29.460
And they're not necessarily like the best teams will always just advance to the finals.

00:09:29.980 --> 00:09:31.920
Yeah. So that, that was, that was really cool.

00:09:32.020 --> 00:09:33.940
And I liked that there was a password system for that.

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:42.940
It reminded me of like having, uh, a bunch of N64 sports games that I love and that you, if you didn't have a memory card, you could use a password for those to store your season.

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:45.100
I did that with Wayne Gretzky's 3D hockey a lot.

00:09:45.340 --> 00:09:46.160
Do love that game.

00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:52.140
That's a great game. Uh, so, so that's that, but let's, uh, let's actually get into the gameplay of baseball here.

00:09:52.340 --> 00:09:53.700
We always start with batting.

00:09:53.880 --> 00:10:00.960
So, uh, who can talk me through kind of, you know, what makes this batting different than the other four, the three games we played?

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:03.740
Um, do you want to go?

00:10:03.860 --> 00:10:18.680
It doesn't matter. I don't have, I mean, I can start, I guess, but the one, the one biggest difference that I noticed was that, uh, I personally relied a lot more on the shadow of the ball, uh, to, to see like the, to see how high or low it was pitched when I, you know, when I'm batting.

00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:30.220
And then, uh, and then I also, like we said, like the, the catcher's mitt do feel like it was easier than, um, RBI baseball to tell when to, to, to read the pitch.

00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:44.720
See, I, so the fact that we've completely switched the, the camera system here and again, camera loose term, but with the perspective now behind the pitcher, uh, at a sort of, uh, at an off angle.

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:55.980
So that there's a lot more depth that needs to be communicated to you in some other way than just a straight line from the pitcher to the hitter.

00:10:56.380 --> 00:11:01.700
Um, the fact that all of these changes were made really threw me off in the beginning.

00:11:01.700 --> 00:11:10.820
Like I was swinging at air half the time, um, because the, the, you would think that it's coming at a low, uh, a low angle, but it's actually way above you.

00:11:10.820 --> 00:11:16.300
But you do eventually learn, um, and if this is your first game, then I'm sure it'll be a lot easier to pick up.

00:11:17.900 --> 00:11:32.740
Um, but the biggest difference between this game and the previous baseball games, aside from the perspective change, is that this is zone hitting as opposed to just move your guy around in the, in the batter's box and try and follow the ball.

00:11:33.680 --> 00:11:37.200
So if it looks like it's coming low, you can decide to swing low.

00:11:37.320 --> 00:11:42.360
If it looks like it's coming high, you can decide to swing high and all of the other permutations of that.

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:44.120
Yeah, that's true. I forgot about that.

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:45.660
And that made, that made a big difference.

00:11:45.660 --> 00:11:50.800
And also I will also say, I feel like the ball, uh, the pitch comes in slower.

00:11:51.260 --> 00:11:54.660
Yeah. It seems like so it's, so it's, that's my big reason why I think it's easier to read.

00:11:55.200 --> 00:12:13.620
Yeah. Uh, I don't really have much more to add to that, but I did think that it was interesting that there were times where even if you, um, even if you could predict what, you know, which zone the pitcher was pitching in, uh, I felt like you still couldn't make exact contact with the ball.

00:12:13.620 --> 00:12:17.800
Uh, if you chose like a, you know, a near area to it.

00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:26.320
So like if he was pitching high and you chose to swing high, you would see it, but it wouldn't necessarily always guarantee contact just because you chose correctly.

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:28.540
And then sometimes it would even just give you a foul.

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:40.400
Right. And I, and I thought that was, I thought that was kind of a strange choice here because there's already so many like permutations involved there of just like, well, there's eight different zones and, uh, or nine, there's nine different zones.

00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:47.960
And now I have to choose like nine different potential batting swings and guessing the right one should give like some kind of extra reward.

00:12:47.960 --> 00:12:55.120
I feel like it's kind of like in, um, the show you have the ability to kind of select like, I think he'll shoot here.

00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:58.260
And if, and if he does, they'll show you the pitch coming as like a reward.

00:12:58.300 --> 00:13:07.180
I think it does enough, uh, to reward you for guessing correctly, because if you guess wrong, you're not going to make any contact.

00:13:07.180 --> 00:13:11.840
So if you get, if you guess correctly, or you're not even guessing, you're just using the information provided.

00:13:12.200 --> 00:13:20.600
Uh, it really is just up to maybe, uh, it is up to the stats of the hitter and, uh, your timing.

00:13:20.860 --> 00:13:25.620
For me, I just had a little problem with like hitting in this game compared to our other baseball games.

00:13:25.800 --> 00:13:28.320
I, it was very hard to get on base.

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:31.740
Uh, but you know, it, it was what it was.

00:13:31.880 --> 00:13:39.760
And I did like the, the added bonus of controlling where you swing instead of moving around in the batter's box, which is something we said we were doing a lot of baseball.

00:13:40.120 --> 00:13:44.160
And in this one, you're more trying to just think about like where you should be making contact.

00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:49.040
You can also do like, um, for bunts and stuff like that.

00:13:49.120 --> 00:13:50.980
You can, or even check swings and stuff.

00:13:51.060 --> 00:13:53.880
I thought that was interesting that you don't necessarily have to commit through.

00:13:54.100 --> 00:13:55.920
You can kind of do like a drag bunt too.

00:13:55.920 --> 00:14:04.300
Yeah. So there, there's, there's a lot of interesting stuff going on there with batting that they've added, uh, that could go either way for me.

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:08.680
You know, I think at times, no, seriously, I think at times I really liked having this setup.

00:14:09.060 --> 00:14:12.560
And then at other times I thought like, well, maybe it was better when it was simpler.

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:15.260
Okay. Uh, okay, cool.

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:16.460
Let's talk about running.

00:14:19.040 --> 00:14:25.680
So with running, um, as soon as you make contact with the ball, uh, there is a, like a flash to black.

00:14:25.680 --> 00:14:32.900
And then you come on to a bit more recognizable screen, uh, of the full diamond and outfield.

00:14:33.540 --> 00:14:38.760
Um, and if you didn't quite hit it right, you're just going to get a, an error sound,

00:14:38.900 --> 00:14:44.200
the kind of, and it's just going to say foul, by the way, the presentation, this is a voiced

00:14:44.200 --> 00:14:45.520
announcer. Right.

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:48.240
Yeah. We've heard them try to do that before.

00:14:48.620 --> 00:14:50.360
And this is the clearest I can hear.

00:14:50.640 --> 00:14:54.520
Great. Yeah. Um, I surprised that we, that we forgot to mention that.

00:14:54.840 --> 00:14:59.000
Yeah. We were busy talking about the television style, but I mean, to have an actual like commentary

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:00.380
was cool. But yeah.

00:15:00.780 --> 00:15:07.660
And with it in hitting as well, like it'll say strike ball, um, foul.

00:15:07.660 --> 00:15:15.380
So you've gotten to contact and now you're on that screen and it's kind of, it's, it's

00:15:15.380 --> 00:15:20.020
similar to the previous games, but this similar to RBI baseball, except for it just maps them

00:15:20.020 --> 00:15:28.100
differently. Um, the, um, the, uh, sprites that are used in, as like the players in this game though,

00:15:28.620 --> 00:15:32.240
uh, retain a more realistic form factor.

00:15:32.500 --> 00:15:35.860
Um, and I think they're just a little bit, uh, snappier too.

00:15:36.060 --> 00:15:38.380
Like there's, there's some momentum to the, to the players.

00:15:38.620 --> 00:15:44.360
Sure. I actually would argue that, uh, in terms of both running fielding and just like the way

00:15:44.360 --> 00:15:49.200
that the game plays out, it's much more realistic in terms of like, you know, we talked

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:53.360
about in the last episode, if you go get, get a ground ball to shortstop, you might make

00:15:53.360 --> 00:15:55.580
it in this one, you don't have a chance, you know, it's done.

00:15:55.720 --> 00:15:59.840
It's a done deal. You also have less time than you think overall when it comes to running.

00:16:00.060 --> 00:16:02.620
So you can't always, yeah, yeah.

00:16:02.780 --> 00:16:06.860
You can't always like, uh, just, just go to second because it was out, you know, it

00:16:06.860 --> 00:16:08.120
was into the outfield. Honestly.

00:16:08.760 --> 00:16:14.740
Yeah, it's true. Um, and I just wanted to say that the umpires in this game are bullshit.

00:16:15.180 --> 00:16:19.180
Uh, they are liars and they're cheats because I've seen it.

00:16:19.200 --> 00:16:24.100
I've definitely had my foot on one or two plates that are just called out.

00:16:24.500 --> 00:16:27.360
And I don't know if you guys had a similar experience.

00:16:27.400 --> 00:16:29.540
Was that yuck, dumb, boo, or bum?

00:16:29.760 --> 00:16:31.040
I think it may have been yuck.

00:16:31.200 --> 00:16:36.540
Okay. For those of you unaware, those are the names provided of the, uh, of the umpires on the

00:16:36.540 --> 00:16:41.760
scoreboard. Uh, yuck was the home plate umpire and he is the ultimate like controller of the game.

00:16:41.920 --> 00:16:45.100
So yeah, he's, he's actually have a similar experience.

00:16:45.220 --> 00:16:47.140
He is master hand in the Super Smash Brothers.

00:16:47.140 --> 00:16:52.360
Uh, I don't know if I had a, I think I would say that in this situation, the phrase tie

00:16:52.360 --> 00:16:53.920
goes to the runner is not true.

